issue_context.body:
As a part of changing primary keys, we will need to recreate all secondary indexes for a given table, which will replace any existing secondary indexes. Unfortunately, due to a bug in our declarative schema changer implementation, we can disable the old secondary indexes before the new ones are ready, leading to severe performance implications in some cases. A simple scenario to reproduce this is:

```
CREATE TABLE public.t1 (
      n INT8 NOT NULL,
      j INT8 NOT NULL,
      CONSTRAINT t1_pkey PRIMARY KEY (n ASC),
      INDEX a (j ASC)
  );
  CREATE TABLE public.t2 (
      n INT8 NOT NULL,
      c INT8 NULL,
      CONSTRAINT t2_pkey PRIMARY KEY (n ASC)
  );
```

Executing a simple swap:
```
explain (DDL) alter table t1 alter primary key using columns (j);
```

We get the following in the plan:
```
  Schema change plan for ALTER TABLE ‹defaultdb›.public.‹t1› ALTER PRIMARY KEY USING COLUMNS (‹j›);
   ├── StatementPhase
   │    └── Stage 1 of 1 in StatementPhase
   │         ├── 8 elements transitioning toward PUBLIC
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → BACKFILL_ONLY PrimaryIndex:{DescID: 104 (t1), IndexID: 7 (t1_pkey+), ConstraintID: 6, TemporaryIndexID: 8, SourceIndexID: 1 (t1_pkey-)}
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → PUBLIC        IndexColumn:{DescID: 104 (t1), ColumnID: 1 (n), IndexID: 7 (t1_pkey+)}
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → PUBLIC        IndexColumn:{DescID: 104 (t1), ColumnID: 2 (j), IndexID: 7 (t1_pkey+)}
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → PUBLIC        IndexData:{DescID: 104 (t1), IndexID: 7 (t1_pkey+)}
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → BACKFILL_ONLY SecondaryIndex:{DescID: 104 (t1), IndexID: 3 (a+), ConstraintID: 2, TemporaryIndexID: 4, SourceIndexID: 1 (t1_pkey-)}
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → PUBLIC        IndexName:{DescID: 104 (t1), Name: "a", IndexID: 3 (a+)}
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → PUBLIC        IndexColumn:{DescID: 104 (t1), ColumnID: 2 (j), IndexID: 3 (a+)}
   │         │    └── ABSENT → PUBLIC        IndexData:{DescID: 104 (t1), IndexID: 3 (a+)}
   │         ├── 5 elements transitioning toward TRANSIENT_ABSENT
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → DELETE_ONLY   TemporaryIndex:{DescID: 104 (t1), IndexID: 8, ConstraintID: 7, SourceIndexID: 1 (t1_pkey-)}
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → PUBLIC        IndexColumn:{DescID: 104 (t1), ColumnID: 1 (n), IndexID: 8}
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → PUBLIC        IndexColumn:{DescID: 104 (t1), ColumnID: 2 (j), IndexID: 8}
   │         │    ├── ABSENT → DELETE_ONLY   TemporaryIndex:{DescID: 104 (t1), IndexID: 4, ConstraintID: 3, SourceIndexID: 1 (t1_pkey-)}
   │         │    └── ABSENT → PUBLIC        IndexColumn:{DescID: 104 (t1), ColumnID: 2 (j), IndexID: 4}
   │         ├── 1 element transitioning toward ABSENT
   **│         │    └── PUBLIC → VALIDATED     SecondaryIndex:{DescID: 104 (t1), IndexID: 2 (a-)}**
```

Which will end up disabling the secondary index during the pre-commit phase


Jira issue: CRDB-31752
